        "falsification": "Compare guarded correction with both controls on paired switch, stable-context, and frozen B-recall observations, and compare each required campaign measure with the mixture. Little improvement over unguarded correction weighs against the guard; a required mean accuracy more than two percentage points below the mixture fails the operational criterion.",
        "hypothesis": "Attenuating retrospective correction when a hidden-state switch is probable may reduce contamination across switches while retaining the benefit of later evidence. Alternatively, correction may have little effect, amplify noise, or be dominated by the underlying memory and routing machinery.",
        "limitations": "In the local paired tests, guarded and unguarded correction produced nearly identical accuracy, offering little support for a benefit from the guard; no-correction sometimes performed better. These exploratory streams do not establish statistical significance or the campaign outcome. The switch probability relies on learned predictions and a fixed hazard, and the approximate correction can still misassign observations. No supervisor development or evaluation comparison was run.",
